                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1055-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>beyerdynamic TG-X 930</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The TG-X 930 is a handheld condenser microphone intended for stage vocal use.</p><p>Unusually for a handheld mic, the element is a &#8220;true condenser&#8221; rather than an electret design. The capsule is the same as in the MC 930 pencil condenser.</p><p>It produces a fixed cardioid pickup pattern, which is very consistent across the frequency range. The pattern deepens slightly above and below 1kHz, but does <em>not</em> become omnidirectional at low frequencies nor hypercardioid at high frequencies. </p><p>Its frequency response graph shows a +4dB presence peak at 10kHz, as well as proximity effect below 400Hz within 10cm of the sound source.</p><p>The headbasket assembly is a multilayer metal-mesh design, with no foam. Beneath the removable grille is a separate, detachable pop shield. The manual advises that both pieces can be cleaned under running water (although not in the dishwasher).</p><p>The mic&#8217;s capsule and electronics are shockmounted to reduce handling noise.</p><p>The mic is available in two colors: matte black (model TG-X 930) and satin nickel (model TG-X 930 Ni). Both ship with a black mic clip (p/n MKV 8).<p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/beyerdynamic/TG-X-930">beyerdynamic TG-X-930</a></p>
